<Base title="Where distributed PostgreSQL work runs" description="Trace partition pruning, tenant routing and colocated joins, then examine Citus transactions, constraints, movement and compatibility boundaries.">
<article class="visual-essay relational-design">
 <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>Where distributed PostgreSQL work runs</h1><div class="essay-meta"><span>PostgreSQL 18 · Citus 14.2</span><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/architecture/">Inside PostgreSQL ↗</a></div></header>
 <p>A table can be divided into pieces without moving any work to another machine. PostgreSQL's native partitions are a good example: each child has its own storage and indexes, while the same server can still own every child. To spread writes across servers, something must also decide which server owns each row and how a query reaches it.</p>
 <p>That decision becomes much easier when the rows needed together have the same owner. Imagine a service for independent shops. Each shop is a tenant, and most requests ask for one tenant's orders and customers. Keeping those related rows together allows a worker to answer the request using an ordinary local PostgreSQL join. A request spanning several owners has a different execution and transaction problem.</p>
 <p>We will first distinguish native partitions from remote tables, then follow a tenant through Citus, an extension that distributes PostgreSQL tables. The native SQL and constraint experiments were checked on PostgreSQL 18.6 and are available in the browser lessons. The distributed diagrams explain Citus 14.2's documented architecture; they are not measurements from a running Citus cluster.</p>
