 <section id="partitions"><h2>Native partitioning and partition pruning</h2>
  <p>Our event table has four rows: IDs 1 and 2 in October, IDs 3 and 4 in November. We partition by <code>occurred_on</code>, creating one child for each month's date range. The parent is the query and routing surface; its child relations hold the actual rows. <code>tableoid::regclass</code> reveals the child containing a selected row.</p>
  <p>An October predicate can exclude November because no November row can satisfy it. This is <strong>partition pruning</strong>. It removes irrelevant relations before their contents need scanning. An index inside October may still help find matching rows there; partition selection and access within the chosen partition are separate steps.</p>
  <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-runtime-pruning">prepared-plan lesson</a> creates this fixture and forces a generic plan so that the dates remain parameters. The two <code>$</code> placeholders receive their values when we execute the prepared statement:</p>
  <Sql code={prepare} label="Prepare a parameterized date query, then execute it for October" />
  <p>Our plan retained an Append operation, which combines child results, but reported <code>Subplans Removed: 1</code>. Only <code>pg_scale_oct</code> was scanned. Reusing the same prepared statement with November dates scanned <code>pg_scale_nov</code> instead. The planner did not have to know October at preparation time: execution-time pruning could use the supplied bounds.</p>
  <p>Now ask only for <code>tenant_id = 7</code>. Tenant 7 has an event in each month, so the date bounds cannot exclude either child. Both are visited. More partitions do not automatically make a query more selective; they help when the query exposes a useful relation to the partition key. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-partitioning.html#DDL-PARTITION-PRUNING">pruning documentation</a> explains the planning and execution stages.</p>
  <p>Partitioning also affects what a key can promise. We declare <code>PRIMARY KEY (occurred_on, event_id)</code>. That permits event ID 1 on October 10 and again on November 15: the complete pairs differ. Each child's unique index can enforce its own entries, but no one child can prove that an ID is absent from every other child. Native partitioned unique constraints therefore need the partition key. If the business requires one global event ID, adding the date to the key has changed the rule rather than enforced it.</p>
  </section>

 <section id="foreign-tables"><h2>Foreign tables and remote transactions</h2>
  <p>The partitioned example above still runs within one PostgreSQL server. You can place foreign tables under a partitioned parent, but connecting remote children adds another mechanism. The bundled <code>postgres_fdw</code> extension sends work to foreign PostgreSQL servers, and <code>EXPLAIN VERBOSE</code> can show the remote SQL. Suitable predicates and joins on the same foreign server can be pushed down; arbitrary pieces on different servers do not become one local join.</p>
  <p>FDW also owns remote transaction boundaries. A local Read Committed transaction uses a Repeatable Read transaction on each accessed remote server. Two local SELECTs can therefore obtain newer local snapshots while an existing remote transaction retains its earlier view. And <code>postgres_fdw</code> cannot prepare its remote transactions for two-phase commit. A collection of writable foreign partitions is not, by itself, an atomic distributed transaction system. These are documented <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/postgres-fdw.html#FDW-TRANSACTION-MANAGEMENT">FDW transaction semantics</a>, not properties to infer from the appearance of one parent table.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="tenant"><h2>Tenant keys and relationships</h2>
  <p>Before choosing machines, make the relationships unambiguous. Tenant 7 has customer 1, Ada; tenant 8 also has a customer 1, Bo. Order 101 belongs to tenant 7 and its customer 1. Order 102 belongs to tenant 8 and its customer 1. The numbers are local identities within a tenant, so a customer reference needs both parts:</p>
  <Sql code={tenantKeys} label="A customer's identity includes its tenant" />
  <p>The foreign key protects <code>(tenant_id, customer_id)</code>. If only tenant 8 has customer 9, an attempted order for <code>(7, 9)</code> fails. The existence of <code>(8, 9)</code> cannot satisfy it. In the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-tenant-keys">tenant-key lesson</a>, PostgreSQL rejects that write with SQLSTATE <code>23503</code>; adding tenant 7's own customer 9 allows the retry.</p>
  <p>A query still has to express the same relationship. Joining only on <code>customer_id</code> makes each order match both Ada and Bo, producing four rows from two orders. A foreign key does not silently fix an incomplete SELECT. Carry both equalities into the query:</p>
  <Sql code={tenantQuery} label="Ask for tenant 7 and join its complete customer identity" />
  <p>The answer is order 101, Ada, total 25. We have established two things before distributing data: how to identify the correct customer, and which tenant's rows are sufficient for this request. The first protects meaning; the second gives a distributed planner a useful boundary.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="routing"><h2>Citus routing and colocated joins</h2>
  <p>Citus adds distribution metadata, planning and execution to PostgreSQL. With hash distribution on <code>tenant_id</code>, it hashes a tenant value to a token, finds the shard range containing that token, then finds that shard's worker placement. A shard is stored as a real PostgreSQL table on a worker, with local indexes, row versions and WAL.</p>
  <p>Distributing both customers and orders by tenant is not sufficient if matching ranges are placed independently. <strong>Co-location</strong> records that their matching shards belong together. The same tenant then reaches both table fragments on one worker, and movement preserves the group. Citus's <code>create_distributed_table</code> supports an explicit <code>colocate_with</code> relation; the declarations and required table constraints belong to a Citus installation, not to ordinary PostgreSQL or this page's browser lesson.</p>
  <Diagram kind="placement" />
  <p>For the query above, the planner has the literal tenant 7 and equality between both tables' tenant columns. It can select the corresponding colocated shards and send their join to one worker. That worker uses its own PostgreSQL planner and executor to find order 101 and customer Ada. The coordinator does not need to fetch every tenant's customers and join them in application memory.</p>
  <p>The placement diagram deliberately avoids claiming that tenant 7 hashes to a particular real shard number. Its two workers show the relationship we require. In a real deployment, several tenants usually share a hash range. A shard is a movement and placement unit; a tenant is an application identity. They are not interchangeable.</p>
  <p>Now remove the tenant restriction and ask for <code>sum(total)</code>. Worker A can contribute 25 and worker B 40; a combining stage adds those partial sums to produce 65. This query benefits from distributing the scan, but it must contact both owners and wait for their work. A cross-tenant join may require repartitioning or other movement of intermediate rows, and some query shapes are restricted. A fast single-tenant plan is not evidence that every query over the same tables is local.</p>
  <p>Keys follow the placement boundary too. Citus requires a distributed table's unique constraints to include its distribution column. If workers A and B each see only their own customers, neither can independently enforce a globally unique email address that omits tenant identity. <code>UNIQUE (tenant_id, email)</code> enforces uniqueness inside a tenant; it does not enforce one email across the entire service. A global rule needs an owner or coordination mechanism capable of seeing every conflicting claim.</p>
  <p>Colocated foreign keys likewise carry the distribution identity. That lets a worker check the referenced tenant row locally. Small shared lookup tables, such as tax categories, can instead be <strong>reference tables</strong>, replicated to workers. Distributed tables can reference them, but updates to a reference table involve its copies rather than one tenant owner. Large or frequently changed tables are therefore a different fit from small shared definitions. The <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/postgresql/citus/reference-ddl?view=citus-14">Citus 14 DDL reference</a> describes these constraints and distribution choices.</p>
  <p>The practical consequence is visible in the SQL itself. Adding an explicit tenant equality supplies both correct identity and a route to local work. Omitting it can change the answer, execution location, or supported plan. Citus's <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/postgresql/citus/reference-workarounds?view=citus-14">SQL support reference</a> distinguishes operations such as single-shard locking reads from their cross-node forms.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="transactions"><h2>Distributed transactions and two-phase commit</h2>
  <p>A transaction confined to one worker can use that worker's PostgreSQL transaction machinery. Suppose instead that an administrative operation must insert one row for tenant 7 and another for tenant 8, and the tenants occupy different workers. One connection now has work in two underlying transactions.</p>
  <p>Committing A and then simply hoping that B commits leaves an obvious failure window. In a two-phase protocol, each worker first <strong>prepares</strong> its transaction: it durably records enough state to finish later, while retaining the transaction's locks. Only after participants are prepared does the coordinator record its commit decision and instruct them to commit their prepared transactions.</p>
  <Diagram kind="commit" />
  <p>If the coordinator fails after recording commit but before B hears it, the durable decision still says B must commit. Recovery can compare outstanding prepared transactions with the recorded decision and complete the participants. Until that happens, prepared work can retain locks and delay other operations. Looking only for a live client query would miss why those resources remain held.</p>
  <p>There is a subtler limit. A and B do not make their changes visible at exactly the same instant. A concurrent query that takes a snapshot after A commits and before B commits can see one of the two new rows. The eventual all-or-none outcome and a single consistent snapshot spanning workers are different guarantees. Citus's <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/citusdata/citus/v14.2.0/src/backend/distributed/README.md">14.2 transaction architecture</a> explicitly distinguishes distributed atomicity from distributed snapshot isolation.</p>
  <p>This matters for our all-tenant sum. Dividing the arithmetic into partial sums is straightforward; deciding which moment those sums describe requires the system's distributed isolation rules. If a business invariant needs a single consistent cross-owner view, do not derive it merely from the fact that the writes used two-phase commit.</p>
  <p>For this two-owner write sequence, Citus 14.2 activates two-phase commit when the modifying transaction expands to another worker. Its <a href="https://github.com/citusdata/citus/blob/v14.2.0/src/backend/distributed/executor/adaptive_executor.c#L3097-L3143">executor implementation</a> traces exactly that case: a first single-shard write, another write on the same connection, then a write on a second worker. A read on that second worker does not create the same modification requirement. The <a href="https://github.com/citusdata/citus/blob/v14.2.0/src/backend/distributed/transaction/remote_transaction.c#L979-L1041">prepare implementation</a> prepares the connections that changed data or schema, skipping read-only participants. This is versioned implementation evidence; a deployment's failure recovery still needs a real multi-worker test.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="movement"><h2>Shard rebalancing and large tenants</h2>
  <p>Suppose tenant 7 grows to produce most of the writes on worker A. Adding worker C creates capacity, but it does not change the existing map from shard ranges to workers. A rebalancer must actually move placements before C owns some of that work.</p>
  <p>With logical shard movement, a destination first receives a copy while the source continues accepting changes. A change stream brings that copy forward. Cutover briefly coordinates writes and changes the placement metadata once the destination can take over. Moving only customers would break the useful locality of our join, so colocated orders and customers move as a group.</p>
  <p>There are two distinct cases. If A has many moderately busy tenants, moving some shard groups can spread their load. If one tenant dominates a shard, Citus can isolate that tenant into a dedicated shard; moving the resulting shard to another worker is a separate operation. Isolation alone does not create a second machine or reduce the tenant's work.</p>
  <p>Even after moving tenant 7 to C, all values with its tenant key still route together. Adding ten workers cannot split one distribution value. If a single tenant exceeds one worker's useful capacity, a finer application boundary may be necessary: for example, independently addressed projects with globally unique project IDs. Then project-local operations can remain local, while tenant-wide reports or relationships may span workers. The new key must match those relationships; changing the hash input alone does not preserve the old guarantees.</p>
  <p>Movement has temporary costs too: a second data copy, change capture and apply, network traffic, and a cutover lock. A move that cannot catch up with incoming writes does not finish merely because its initial copy reached 100%. The <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/postgresql/citus/cluster-management?view=citus-14">Citus cluster-management guide</a> describes worker addition, rebalancing and tenant isolation. The family <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=shard-cutover">cutover model</a> lets you vary a final correction and see why copying rows is insufficient; its SQL tables model ownership rather than running Citus.</p>
  <p>Sharding also creates more histories to protect. Worker replication supplies a replacement for an individual worker; it does not by itself produce a consistent restore point for the entire distributed operation. Citus 14 changed its coordinated restore-point handling to account for commit decisions originating on metadata workers as well as the coordinator. The <a href="https://www.citusdata.com/updates/v14-0/">release explanation</a> shows why independent node backups are not a substitute for coordinating distributed transaction state.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="boundaries"><h2>PostgreSQL compatibility across distributed systems</h2>
  <p>These mechanisms occupy different layers. Native partitions divide relations inside PostgreSQL. FDW accesses separately managed PostgreSQL servers. Citus adds a distributed planning, placement and transaction layer while retaining PostgreSQL workers. A managed Citus service may own provisioning, failover and backup procedures on top of those mechanisms; the extension's documentation alone does not specify the service's operational promises.</p>
  <p>Other distributed databases accept PostgreSQL clients or reuse parts of PostgreSQL while using a different storage and replication engine. YugabyteDB, for example, describes a PostgreSQL-derived YSQL layer over its distributed DocDB storage. Its current <a href="https://docs.yugabyte.com/stable/api/ysql/">YSQL reference</a> names PostgreSQL 15 as that layer's basis, while its <a href="https://docs.yugabyte.com/stable/architecture/">architecture documentation</a> explains the distributed storage beneath it. These are not PostgreSQL 18 heap files made distributed by a connection string.</p>
  <p>Compatibility must therefore be attached to a question: will this driver connect, will this query run, will this extension load, does this isolation level provide the required invariant, and who recovers a failed participant? The answers can differ. A familiar SQL spelling does not justify applying PostgreSQL's physical page inspection, WAL failover procedure or Citus's tenant-locality rules to another engine.</p>
  <p>For the shop service, the useful design starts with the requests we followed: one tenant's order and customer can share an owner; an all-tenant sum contacts several; a global uniqueness rule needs a global authority; and a tenant too large for one worker needs a finer boundary. PostgreSQL gives us concrete local mechanisms. The distribution system determines how those mechanisms cooperate when an operation crosses the boundary.</p>
 </section>
